<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 4.7 Extreme Values and Saddle Points.

  Definitions, theorems, summaries, and problem statements are kept;
  derivations and solutions are replaced by blank workspace boxes, each
  with a one-line statement of what has to be done.  Content mirrors
  extreme-values-saddle-points.ptx of the full lecture notes; every
  xml:id here is prefixed with "skel-" so the two can coexist.

  Figures: every video is kept, since the surfaces cannot be drawn by
  hand and a reader checks their classification against them.  The
  prefigure diagrams that set a problem up (the open disk, the traces of
  the saddle, the rectangle with its four edges, the stability
  cross-sections) are reproduced in full.  The plot of the critical
  points and the plot of the candidate points ARE the answers to their
  examples, so those two become empty grids to mark up.
-->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-fmv-extreme-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Extreme Values and Saddle Points</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand what it means for a function of two variables to have a local
maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point, and visualize each on the
surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Recognize critical points as the candidates for local extrema, and understand
why a vanishing gradient is necessary but not sufficient for an extremum.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand the idea behind the Second Derivative Test and what it reveals
about the local shape of a surface near a critical point.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Distinguish local extrema from absolute extrema, and understand why the
absolute extrema of a function on a closed, bounded region may occur on the
boundary.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret extreme values in a physical setting, such as the equilibrium and
stability of a system.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-local-extrema">
<title>Local Maximum and Local Minimum</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-local-extrema">
<title>Local Maximum and Local Minimum</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let the domain of the function <m>f(x,y)</m> contain the point
<m>(a,b)</m>. Then
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>f(a,b)</m> is a <term>local minimum</term> if
<m>f(a,b) \le f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> in an open
disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f(a,b)</m> is a <term>local maximum</term> if
<m>f(a,b) \ge f(x,y)</m> for all the points <m>(x,y)</m> in an open
disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Test the definition on two surfaces you already know,
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m> and <m>f(x,y) = -x^2 - y^2</m>, at the origin.
(For each, decide which of the two inequalities of
<xref ref="def-skel-local-extrema"/> holds on a disk around <m>(0,0)</m>,
and say why it holds for <em>every</em> point of the disk rather than
just some. The word <q>local</q> is doing work here: say what would
change if the disk had to be the whole plane.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {$x^2+y^2$ at the origin:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {$-x^2-y^2$ at the origin:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {What &lt;q&gt;local&lt;/q&gt; is doing:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for classifying the origin on each of the two paraboloids and for the role of the word local.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-local-min-video">
<caption>
The paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>. Over an open disk centered at
<m>(0,0)</m>, the value <m>f(0,0) = 0</m> is smaller than every other
value of the function, so <m>f(0,0)</m> is a local minimum.
</caption>
