<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-first-derivative-test">
<title>Theorem I</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> has a local maximum or minimum at an interior point
<m>(a,b)</m> of its domain and if the first partial derivatives exist
there, then
<md xml:id="eq-skel-first-derivative-test" number="yes">
f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Check <xref ref="thm-skel-first-derivative-test"/> on
<m>f(x,y) = x^2+y^2</m>, and then read the theorem carefully in the other
direction. (The check is two derivatives. Then ask: does the theorem say
that a point where both partials vanish <em>must</em> be an extremum? It
does not <mdash/> state which way the implication runs, and why that
matters for how you will use it.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$f_x(0,0) =$ \qquad $f_y(0,0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {The implication runs:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the two partial derivatives at the origin and one for the direction of the implication in the theorem.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-critical-point">
<title>Critical Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
An interior point of the domain of a function <m>f(x,y)</m> where both
<m>f_x</m> and <m>f_y</m> are zero or where one or both of <m>f_x</m>
and <m>f_y</m> do not exist is a <term>critical point</term> of
<m>f</m>. Note that not every critical point is a local extremum.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-saddle-point">
<title>Saddle Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
A differentiable function <m>f(x,y)</m> has a <term>saddle point</term>
at a critical point <m>(a,b)</m> if in every open disk centered at
<m>(a,b)</m> there are domain points <m>(x,y)</m> where
<m>f(a,b) \lt f(x,y)</m> and domain points <m>(x,y)</m> where
<m>f(a,b) \gt f(x,y)</m>. The corresponding point
<m>(a,b,f(a,b))</m> on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> is called a
saddle point of the surface.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-saddle-example">
<title>A Saddle Point Example</title>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-saddle-point">
<title>Saddle Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<md xml:id="eq-skel-saddle-function" number="yes">
f(x,y) = \frac{y^2}{3} - \frac{x^2}{3}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Show that <m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(0,0)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(This is the hyperbolic paraboloid of
<xref ref="subsec-skel-hyperbolic-paraboloid"/>. Find the partials and
confirm that <m>(0,0)</m> is the only critical point. Then, to match
<xref ref="def-skel-saddle-point"/>, you need points of <em>both</em>
kinds in every disk around the origin: restrict <m>f</m> to each
coordinate axis in turn and look at the sign. Note at the end that both
partials vanish here and yet there is no extremum.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$f_x =$ \qquad $f_y =$ \qquad critical point:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {Along the $x$-axis: $f(x,0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {Along the $y$-axis: $f(0,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Conclusion:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the partial derivatives and critical point, for the function along each coordinate axis, and for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-saddle-video">
<caption>
The hyperbolic paraboloid <m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m>. Along the
<m>x</m>-axis the surface falls below the origin, and along the
<m>y</m>-axis it rises above the origin, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle
point.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-saddle-point"
       source="videos/saddle_point_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-saddle-traces">
<caption>
The traces of <m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m> along the two coordinate planes.
In the plane <m>y = 0</m> the trace <m>z = -x^2/3</m> opens downward,
while in the plane <m>x = 0</m> the trace <m>z = y^2/3</m> opens
upward, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="skel-prefigure-saddle-traces">
<diagram dimensions="(360,280)" margins="10">
<definition>up(t) = t^2/3</definition>
<definition>down(t) = -t^2/3</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.4,-1.7,2.4,1.7)">
<axes at="sd-axes" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<graph at="sd-up" function="up" domain="(-2.1,2.1)" stroke="blue"
       thickness="2.5"/>
<graph at="sd-down" function="down" domain="(-2.1,2.1)" stroke="red"
       thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="sd-saddle" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<label at="sd-up-label" anchor="(1.45,0.95)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>z=\dfrac{y^2}{3}</m>
</label>
<label at="sd-down-label" anchor="(1.45,-0.95)" alignment="sw" color="red">
<m>z=-\dfrac{x^2}{3}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two parabolas through the origin showing the traces of the hyperbolic paraboloid z equals y squared over 3 minus x squared over 3. The blue parabola opens upward and is the trace along the y axis. The red parabola opens downward and is the trace along the x axis. The origin, where they meet, is the saddle point.">
  <annotation ref="sd-up" text="The blue parabola z equals y squared over 3, the trace in the plane x equals 0, which opens upward, so the function is positive along the y axis."/>
  <annotation ref="sd-down" text="The red parabola z equals negative x squared over 3, the trace in the plane y equals 0, which opens downward, so the function is negative along the x axis."/>
  <annotation ref="sd-saddle" text="The origin, where the two traces meet. The function increases in one direction and decreases in the other, so this is a saddle point."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-second-derivative-test">
<title>The Second Derivative Test</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-second-derivative-test">
<title>Theorem II (The Second Derivative Test)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>f(x,y)</m> and its first and second partial derivatives
are continuous throughout a disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m> and that
<m>f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0</m>. Then
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="i.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>local maximum</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx} \lt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \gt 0</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>local minimum</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx} \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \gt 0</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>saddle point</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \lt 0</m> at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The test is inconclusive</em> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 0</m> at <m>(a,b)</m>. In this case, we
must find some other way to determine the behavior of <m>f</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
The expression <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</m> is called the
<term>discriminant</term> or the <term>Hessian</term> of <m>f(x,y)</m>,
and it is easiest to remember as a determinant:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-hessian" number="yes">
H(x,y) =
\begin{vmatrix}
f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\
f_{xy} \amp f_{yy}
\end{vmatrix}
= f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Before using it, get the shape of the test clear. (Which quantity is
consulted <em>first</em>, and what does its sign decide? Only in one of
those branches does a second quantity get consulted <mdash/> which, and
what does it decide there? Note also which entry of
<xref ref="eq-skel-hessian"/> is used twice, and why
<xref ref="thm-skel-mixed-derivative"/> permits that.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {First look at: \qquad\qquad which decides:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {Then, if needed, look at: \qquad which decides:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Why $f_{xy}$ may be used for both off-diagonal entries:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the order in which the two quantities of the test are consulted and for why the mixed partial appears twice.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-quartic-extrema">
<title>Finding Local Extrema and Saddle Points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the local maximum and minimum values and saddle points of the
function
<md xml:id="eq-skel-quartic-function" number="yes">
z = f(x,y) = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Set both first partials to zero. The resulting pair is symmetric in
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>; substitute one into the other to get a single
equation in one variable, and factor it completely <mdash/> only the real
roots count. Then form <xref ref="eq-skel-hessian"/> once, as a formula in
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and evaluate it at each critical point rather than
recomputing. Mark the critical points on the grid, which is the
intersection of the two curves you solved.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="52% 44%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (8,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12) -- (8,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (8,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (8,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,14.7) {$f_x =$ \qquad $f_y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,11.7) {Solving:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {$H(x,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,4.2) {Classification of each point:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the first partial derivatives, for solving the system, for the Hessian, and for the classification of each critical point.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-1.6,-1.6) grid[step=0.25] (1.6,1.6);
\draw[->, thick] (-1.6,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1.6) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1} \draw (\x,-0.05) -- (\x,0.05) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,1} \draw (-0.05,\y) -- (0.05,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid from negative one and a half to one and a half in both directions, for sketching the two curves and marking their intersections.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-quartic-video">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy</m> has a saddle point at
<m>(0,0,0)</m> and two local minima, at <m>(1,1,-2)</m> and
<m>(-1,-1,-2)</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-quartic-extrema"
       source="videos/quartic_extrema_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-shortest-distance">
<title>Shortest Distance to a Plane</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the shortest distance from the point <m>(1,0,-2)</m> to the plane
<m>x + 2y + z = 4</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Write the distance from <m>(1,0,-2)</m> to a general point of the
plane using <xref ref="eq-skel-3d-distance"/>, then use the plane's
equation to eliminate one variable. Minimizing <m>d^2</m> instead of
<m>d</m> avoids the square root <mdash/> say why the two have their minimum
at the same place. Find the single critical point and confirm with the
Hessian, then argue that this local minimum is the absolute one. Finally
substitute back to get the distance itself.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,17);
\draw[gray!35] (0,13.5) -- (15,13.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,11) -- (15,11);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.7) {$d^2 = f(x,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.2) {Why minimizing $d^2$ is enough:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Critical point:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {$H =$ \qquad so the point is a:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$d =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in five rows, for the squared distance as a function of two variables, for why squaring is harmless, for the critical point, for the Hessian test, and for the distance.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Maximum and Absolute Minimum</title>

<p>
In the next example, we will see how to find absolute extrema of a
function <m>f(x,y)</m> over a closed bounded region <m>R</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Extrema over a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> on the rectangle
<md xml:id="eq-skel-rectangle-region" number="yes">
R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Three steps. First the critical points of <m>f</m> lying <em>inside</em>
<m>R</m>. Then the boundary, which is not one curve but four edges: on
each, one variable is fixed, so <m>f</m> becomes a one-variable function
on a closed interval <mdash/> handle each with single-variable calculus,
and do not forget the endpoints. Finally compare every value collected.
Note that no second derivative test is needed anywhere here; say why.
Label the four edges on <xref ref="fig-skel-rectangle-region"/> as you
go.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,17) -- (15,17);
\draw[gray!35] (0,13.5) -- (15,13.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {I. Interior critical points:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.7) {II. On $L_1$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.2) {\phantom{II. }On $L_2$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {\phantom{II. }On $L_3$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {\phantom{II. }On $L_4$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {III. Absolute max: \qquad\qquad absolute min:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in six rows, for the interior critical points, for the function restricted to each of the four edges with its extreme values, and for the absolute extrema.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

